00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:03.900
drag this around, or we can choose a setting.

62
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:08.039
Now in this case, I know, I think it's about nine is going to be our exposure.

63
00:04:08.039 --> 00:04:12.039
If that's too much, again, we can take this a little bit lower.

64
00:04:12.039 --> 00:04:17.439
Something to take note of here, for those of you that are diehard camera fans and stuff.

65
00:04:17.439 --> 00:04:22.399
So we are adjusting the exposure of our scene, but because we're working within a digital

66
00:04:22.399 --> 00:04:24.660
medium, this is non-destructive.

67
00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.699
There are some different things we can do, in particular with using cinematic cameras

68
00:04:28.699 --> 00:04:35.300
where we can do a, you know, more filmic or camera-based exposure, but this post-processing

69
00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:39.779
volume, all we're simply doing is just trying to get this to where we can see our scene,

70
00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:42.939
but we absolutely can use EV stops.

71
00:04:42.939 --> 00:04:48.100
We can use any of those types of parameters that we would normally to do this.

72
00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.540
All right, hopefully that makes sense.

73
00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:54.579
One of the other questions that'll probably be here is why did we choose eight for our

74
00:04:54.579 --> 00:04:55.980
exposure compensation?

